      "source": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "field": "hanzi.etymology_notes[2]",
      "text": "顔前也。从𦣻，象人面形。凡面之屬皆从面。",
      "record_character": "面",
      "kind": "imported_excerpt"
    },
    {
      "id": "E4",
      "source": "wiktionary",
      "field": "hanzi.etymology_notes[3]",
      "text": "Pictogram (象形), A man’s face, with an eye exaggerated, derived from 𦣻. The horizontal stroke was added later. Unrelated to 自.\nEtymology 2: Simplified from 麵 (elimination of 麥) as adopted by the Chinese Character Simplification Scheme (《漢字簡化方案》) in 1956.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *sV-merl (“face”); cognate with Mizo hmêl (“face”), Jingpho man (“face”). 偭 (OC *menʔ, “to turn; to violate”) is probably cognate (Wang, 1982), perhaps its endoactive (Schuessler, 2007).",
      "record_character": "面",
      "kind": "imported_excerpt"
    },
